{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-cd41ac91-8d7a-4f89-9e05-bdc85da58b44","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b70e37e4e90dc977","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-s3ih19hh/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-E001.md","content":"# Workspace resolution initialization dependencies in the artifacts-only context\n\n## Description\n\nThis investigation resolves whether the workspace-resolution path used by the MLflow tracking\nserver — the `kubernetes-auth` plugin from `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins`, registered through the\n`mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point group and loaded by `WorkspaceStoreRegistry`, together with\nthe `kubernetes://` workspace store — can be initialized and queried in an artifacts-only server\ncontext where tracking stores and model registry stores are not initialized. The strategy flags\nthis as a genuine unknown in two places (the plugin dependency note and the assumption \"the\nworkspace store provides experiment-to-workspace mapping that is accessible without initializing\ntracking stores — needs validation\"), and the risk table names plugin coupling to full server\ninitialization as a risk whose mitigation requires validating initialization dependencies early.\n\nThe outcome determines the scope of the plugin work (verification and integration only, versus a\nrefactoring that factors workspace resolution into an independent initialization path) and the\ninitialization approach taken by the server-side combined-flag implementation. Both downstream\nwork streams are gated on this investigation.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Map the initialization dependency chain of the workspace-resolution path: plugin entry-point\n  loading via `WorkspaceStoreRegistry`, workspace store construction for the `kubernetes://` URI\n  scheme, and the experiment-to-workspace lookup used for authorization.\n- Determine, for each element of that chain, whether it requires a tracking store, model registry\n  store, or any other artifact of full tracking-server initialization that an artifacts-only server\n  does not initialize.\n- Determine whether experiment-to-workspace mapping lookups function when only the workspace\n  provider is initialized.\n- If any dependency is coupled to full server initialization, produce a concrete factoring plan for\n  an independent initialization path within the plugin (per the strategy's risk mitigation), sized\n  and with identified touch points.\n- Deliverable: a written findings document recording the dependency map, the determination\n  (decoupled vs. coupled), and — if coupled — the refactoring plan.\n\nTwo bounded outcomes:\n\n- **Outcome A (decoupled)**: workspace resolution initializes and resolves without tracking/model\n  registry stores. Downstream plugin work is verification and integration only.\n- **Outcome B (coupled)**: resolution depends on full tracking-server initialization. Downstream\n  plugin work is the independent-initialization-path refactoring.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The findings document enumerates every initialization dependency of the workspace-resolution path\n  (plugin loading, workspace store construction, mapping lookup) and states, for each, whether it is\n  available in an artifacts-only server context, with references to the source locations that\n  evidence the answer.\n- The document states a single determination — outcome A or outcome B — with a reproducible basis\n  (source analysis and, where run locally, an executable demonstration with test doubles).\n- If outcome B, the document contains a factoring plan for an independent initialization path that\n  identifies the code to move or decouple and preserves existing tracking-server behavior.\n- The determination is communicated to the server-side and plugin implementation work streams as a\n  decision record sufficient to start either downstream scope without further analysis.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-7 (P1, auth plugin workspace resolution functions correctly in the artifacts-only context\n  without tracking store initialization): this investigation resolves the unknown that determines\n  how HLR-7 is delivered.\n- Supports HLR-2 (P0, artifact requests validated against workspace membership): the answer gates\n  the workspace-validation capability's initialization approach.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Component mapping: canonical Jira component `MLflow`; the investigation spans the\n  `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` package and the MLflow server's workspace-store initialization code\n  (no separate canonical entries exist for either sub-surface beyond `MLflow`).\n- Scope boundary: this investigation does not design the workspace validation itself, the artifact\n  path resolution strategy, or the routing topology — it answers the initialization-dependency\n  question only.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":72310,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aacdbd101d79c014","agent_id":"ac3af399cc79681ac","session_id":"16006724-b396-4d55-996f-e395dcbbe214","uuid":"d87881e1-cd91-451c-84c0-b25008b95f6e","timestamp":"2026-10-26T13:36:43.228Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009"}
