{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-8454c33e-a2e1-4dc8-a225-9d5a42ba404f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-95042cdda60deb61","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-s3ih19hh/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-E002.md","content":"# Split-server artifact traffic routing design\n\n## Description\n\nThis investigation determines how the MLflow operator should configure artifact traffic routing in\nthe split-server topology: how the main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated\nartifacts-only instance, and whether that routing can be resolved entirely at the Gateway API\n(HTTPRoute) level or requires a configuration change in the tracking server to delegate artifact\noperations. The strategy flags this as an open question owned by the MLflow Team that \"affects\nHTTPRoute design and determines whether the tracking server needs a configuration change to\ndelegate artifact operations.\"\n\nThe outcome determines the routing implementation approach in the operator's split-server topology\nwork and whether an additional tracking-server configuration change is needed.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Analyze how artifact traffic reaches the main tracking server today (artifact proxy endpoints and\n  ingress path structure) and what distinguishes artifact requests from other API traffic at the\n  routing layer.\n- Evaluate candidate routing mechanisms for the split-server topology — in particular Gateway API\n  HTTPRoute-based traffic splitting versus a tracking-server-side delegation configuration —\n  against the constraints that authentication at the ingress level (kube-rbac-proxy sidecar,\n  `--app-name kubernetes-auth`) works identically for both instances and is unchanged.\n- Determine the HTTPRoute design implications for both the main tracking instance and the dedicated\n  artifacts-only instance, consistent with the operator's existing conditional Gateway API CRD\n  discovery pattern.\n- Determine whether the tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact\n  operations to the dedicated instance and, if so, characterize that change at the functional level\n  (what it configures and how it propagates), leaving exact configuration surface naming to the\n  implementing team.\n- Deliverable: a routing design decision document: the selected mechanism, the HTTPRoute design,\n  the determination of whether server-side delegation is required, and the constraints respected.\n\nTwo bounded outcomes:\n\n- **Outcome A (route-level)**: artifact traffic is split at the Gateway/HTTPRoute level; no\n  tracking-server change is needed.\n- **Outcome B (server delegation)**: the tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate\n  artifact operations to the dedicated instance.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The decision document states the selected routing mechanism with the evidence and constraints\n  that motivated it, covering both the main tracking instance and the dedicated artifacts-only\n  instance.\n- The document states a single determination — outcome A or outcome B — for whether tracking-server\n  configuration is required, with the functional characterization of that change if required.\n- The HTTPRoute design is consistent with the operator's existing conditional CRD discovery pattern\n  for Gateway API resources (the operator must keep functioning on clusters without the Gateway API\n  CRDs).\n- The design preserves unchanged authentication behavior at the ingress level for both instances.\n- The determination is communicated as a decision record sufficient for the operator topology work\n  to proceed without further analysis.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-6 (P1, main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only\n  instance): this investigation selects the mechanism by which that HLR is delivered.\n- Supports HLR-5 (P1, operator deploys