{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-b0898dfa-25c0-4f92-a1c7-3027b33dba8f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9292044ccdfb0451","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-s3ih19hh/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-E003.md","content":"# Composable workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow server\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic delivers the server-side half of workspace-aware artifact serving: making the MLflow\ntracking server's `--artifacts-only` mode composable with the existing `--enable-workspaces` flag,\nso that the artifacts-only server initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate\nartifact requests against tenant boundaries. No new server flag is introduced — the workspace-aware\nbehavior emerges from combining existing flags (Staff Engineer / SME Input). When both flags are\nset, the server requires an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`, initializes only the workspace\nprovider, keeps tracking and model registry stores uninitialized, and leaves non-artifact API\nendpoints disabled.\n\nFor each artifact request, the server resolves the artifact path to its parent experiment\n(artifacts are stored under experiment-scoped paths, e.g. `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`),\nlooks up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegates authorization\nto the existing auth plugin infrastructure. Requests that fail workspace validation receive HTTP\n403. The design is fail-closed: if the workspace store is unreachable while workspace-aware mode is\nactive, artifact requests are denied rather than served without validation.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Flag composition: allow `--artifacts-only` to combine with `--enable-workspaces`; when both are\n  set, require an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` (fail to start with a clear error if missing) and\n  initialize only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests, without initializing\n  tracking or model registry stores and without enabling non-artifact API endpoints.\n- Workspace validation in artifact request handling: resolve the artifact path of each request to\n  its parent experiment, look up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and\n  delegate authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure; deny non-members with HTTP 403\n  and an error indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n- Fail-closed behavior: when workspace-aware mode is active and the workspace store is unreachable,\n  deny artifact requests with an error indicating workspace validation is unavailable (HTTP 503),\n  never serve without validation.\n- Backwards compatibility: `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` retains current\n  behavior (artifacts served on path alone, no workspace validation); each flag used individually\n  retains its current behavior; no existing API contracts, CLI arguments, or configuration keys\n  change meaning.\n- Audit logging of workspace access denials through server logs, with the context available at the\n  denial point (workspace, experiment, requesting identity) — the P2 audit requirement is delivered\n  here because it is emitted from the same denial path this epic builds.\n- Short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions per artifact request path, consistent with\n  caching patterns already used in the tracking server's auth path (per the strategy's risk\n  mitigation for per-request resolution latency).\n- Initial artifact-path resolution scoped to the S3 artifact store (the standard RHOAI\n  configuration) with the well-defined `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` structure;\n  limitations for non-standard artifact roots documented.\n- Test coverage: multi-tenant allow/deny scenarios, single-tenant and no-workspace regression,\n  fail-closed edge cases (unreachable workspace store), flag-combination startup matrix.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A server started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://`\n  initializes the workspace provider without initializing tracking or model registry stores, and\n  non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled — verified by startup behavior and endpoint probes.\n- A server started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces` but without `--workspace-store-uri`\n  fails to start with a clear error message.\n- A user who is a member of workspace A requesting an artifact belonging to an experiment in\n  workspace A receives HTTP 200 with the correct artifact content.\n- A user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B requesting an artifact belonging to an\n  experiment in workspace B receives HTTP 403 with an error message indicating insufficient\n  workspace permissions.\n- With the workspace-aware artifacts-only server running and the workspace store (Kubernetes API)\n  unreachable, artifact requests are denied with HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is\n  unavailable (fail-closed).\n- A server started with `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` serves artifacts without\n  workspace validation (HTTP 200 regardless of workspace membership) — current behavior preserved.\n- Each flag used individually retains current behavior, demonstrated by the existing server test\n  suite passing without modification.\n- Workspace access denials are recorded in server logs with the denial context available at the\n  point of denial.\n- Workspace membership decision caching is implemented with a short TTL, and expired entries are\n  re-validated (covered by tests).\n- Artifact path resolution is implemented for the S3-standard `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`\n  layout, and limitations for non-standard artifact roots are documented.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri`.\n- HLR-2 (P0): artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403.\n- HLR-3 (P0): fail-closed behavior when the workspace store is unreachable.\n- HLR-4 (P0): single-tenant / no-`--enable-workspaces` deployments retain current behavior.\n- HLR-8 (P2, bundled): audit logging for workspace access denials through server logs — bundled\n  rather than split because denial logging is emitted from the same workspace-validation denial path\n  this epic builds for HLR-2/HLR-3; a separate epic would make two passes over the same call sites\n  and has no independent user value apart from the validation feature.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- This epic is gated by the workspace-resolution initialization-dependency investigation: its\n  workspace-provider initialization approach follows that investigation's determination. If\n  workspace resolution proves coupled to full tracking-server initialization, the initialization\n  path here is reworked to use the independent initialization path delivered by the plugin\n  refactoring before workspace validation can land.\n- The workspace validation delegates to the existing auth plugin infrastructure\n  (`--app-name kubernetes-auth`); this epic does not prescribe Kubernetes API authorization\n  mechanics, which remain an mlflow-kubernetes-plugins implementation detail (Staff Engineer /\n  SME Input).\n- The acceptable per-request latency target is an open question owned by PM/Engineering; caching is\n  implemented per the strategy's risk mitigation regardless, so the open answer trims or keeps scope\n  within this epic rather than changing structure.\n- Component mapping: canonical Jira component `MLflow`.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":74289,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aacdbd101d79c014","agent_id":"ac3af399cc79681ac","session_id":"16006724-b396-4d55-996f-e395dcbbe214","uuid":"b4f4d853-882d-4a49-bd8a-a53e11686540","timestamp":"2026-10-26T13:37:18