oyment's resources.\n- A CR configuration surface (within the existing MLflow CR) that enables the split-server\n  artifacts-only deployment — exact field naming is an implementing-team decision; no new\n  server-level flag or workspace-aware CR field is introduced (Staff Engineer / SME Input).\n- Propagation of workspace configuration to the artifacts-only instance using the existing\n  configuration mechanisms, so the dedicated instance runs with the combined flag set\n  (`--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes:// --app-name\n  kubernetes-auth --serve-artifacts`).\n- Configuration of the main tracking deployment to route artifact traffic to the dedicated\n  artifacts-only instance, following the routing mechanism selected by the split-server routing\n  design investigation (route-level traffic splitting versus a tracking-server delegation\n  configuration).\n- kube-rbac-proxy sidecar deployed on the artifacts-only instance identically to the main tracking\n  deployment — the authentication layer is unchanged and works identically for both instances.\n- Conditional HTTPRoute creation for the artifacts-only instance based on runtime Gateway API CRD\n  discovery, per the operator's existing optional-dependency pattern.\n- Operator validation that rejects MLflow CR configurations where the artifacts-only instance would\n  be deployed without workspace configuration (misconfiguration that could let artifact requests\n  bypass workspace validation), with a clear error/status condition.\n- Regression coverage: single-deployment (non-split) MLflow CRs reconcile exactly as before;\n  end-to-end coverage for the split topology including artifact traffic routing and workspace\n  validation through the deployed instances.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given an MLflow CR with split-server artifact serving enabled, reconciling the CR creates a\n  dedicated artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute separate from the main tracking\n  Deployment — verified by the presence of distinct Deployment resources with the correct flag\n  combinations.\n- The artifacts-only Deployment's container command includes `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces\n  --workspace-store-uri kubernetes:// --app-name kubernetes-auth --serve-artifacts`, verified by\n  inspecting the rendered Deployment spec.\n- The rendered artifacts-only instance starts successfully with the combined flag set (this gates on\n  the server-side combined-flag capability being available; it is tracked here as an acceptance\n  criterion at execution time rather than a work dependency, so operator and server work proceed in\n  parallel).\n- The dedicated instance runs the kube-rbac-proxy sidecar configured identically to the main\n  tracking deployment, and authentication at the ingress level works identically for both.\n- HTTPRoute resources for the artifacts-only instance are created only when the Gateway API CRDs are\n  discovered at runtime, and the operator continues to function on clusters without them.\n- Artifact traffic sent to the main tracking deployment is served by the dedicated artifacts-only\n  instance with workspace validation applied (end-to-end through the deployed split topology).\n- The operator rejects MLflow CR configurations that enable the artifacts-only instance without\n  workspace configuration, with a clear error or status condition, so misconfiguration cannot\n  silently bypass workspace validation.\n- Single-deployment MLflow CRs reconcile exactly as before (regression suite green).\n- The chart rendering supports the second Deployment within the existing Helm-in-operator chart\n  architecture, or any required chart restructuring is completed within this epic (validating the\n  strategy's assumption).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-5 (P1): operator deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment with its\n  own Service and HTTPRoute.\n- HLR-6 (P1): main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only\n  instance (mechanism selected by the split-server routing design investigation).\n- HLR-4 (P0, regression scope): deployments without the split topology retain current behavior —\n  covered here as CR-driven-deployment regression for the same backwards-compatibility requirement\n  the server epic covers at the flag level.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- This epic is gated by the split-server artifact traffic routing design investigation: the routing\n  implementation follows whichever mechanism that investigation selects. If the investigation\n  determines the tracking server needs a delegation configuration, that server-side change is\n  planned as conditional branch work under the investigation, not folded into this operator epic.\n- Split-deployment operational complexity is a named strategy risk (two Deployments, two Services,\n  two HTTPRoutes, routing configuration). Mitigations in scope: declarative single-CR management and\n  the operator validation acceptance criterion above.\n- No DAG dependency on the server-side combined-flag work: different repositories with no shared\n  build artifacts; the coupling is the acceptance criterion above (\"rendered artifacts-only instance\n  starts successfully with the combined flag set\"), never a blocking edge.\n- Component mapping: canonical Jira component `MLflow` (no canonical \"MLflow Operator\" entry\n  exists; the operator manages MLflow instances, so `MLflow` is the closest canonical name). The\n  epic's work spans the strategy components \"MLflow Operator\" (chart templates, reconciliation,\n  validation) and \"MLflow\" (main tracking deployment routing configuration).\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":75904,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aacdbd101d79c014","agent_id":"ac3af399cc79681ac","session_id":"16006724-b396-4d55-996f-e395dcbbe214","uuid":"a6579a66-9015-4c85-85b8-9ea