or confirms). Critical path length 2 is short for an M-L\nstrategy but reflects rule-mandated parallelism across the three components rather than\nmissing structure; the depth-2 investigation→implementation chains are complete.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\nAll epics carry component `MLflow` (the only canonical entry — verified against\n`.context/rhai-components.txt`, which has no `MLflow Operator` or `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins`\nentries) and team `MLflow Team`, with the mapping constraint logged in the summary and each\nepic's Notes. No cross-team bundling. No scope duplication: the A/B plugin branch epics are\nmutually exclusive alternatives, not parallel builds; E004 (operator-side routing\npropagation) and BRANCH-B-E006 (server-side delegation surface) split by codebase with the\nboundary explicitly recorded in E004's gate_failure_impact. No work-product type mixing.\nE003 is at the ~2-week guideline (strategy estimates server-side work at 1-2 sprints) but is\none logical change over a single request-handling surface with the HLR-8 bundling rationale\nrecorded — watch note only, no split demanded. E004, however, is sized at 2-3 sprints by the\nstrategy's own estimate (split-deployment topology) with no size Health Warning — see the\nminor issue below.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\nBoth Investigations are genuine: E001's decoupled/coupled determination selects between the\ntwo plugin branch epics and rewrites E003's initialization approach; E002's route-level vs.\nserver-delegation determination selects whether BRANCH-B-E006 exists and rewrites E004's\nrouting implementation. Both produce concrete decision-record deliverables and both have\ndownstream dependents. All five gated epics have `gated_by` set to an Investigation that\nappears in their direct `dependencies` list, and all have `gate_failure_impact` with both\n`action` (rewrite/remove) and a substantive `fallback_approach` — true gates, not scheduling\ndependencies. No epic lists an Investigation dependency without `gated_by`. Implementation\nepics produce concrete artifacts (server capability, operator topology, plugin changes).\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\nType-appropriate signal fields throughout: E001/E002 carry `investigation_signals`\n(5-signal rubric), all five Implementation epics carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric), and\nno epic mixes the two. All seven ai-signals rationale files exist and their tables match the\nfrontmatter values exactly. Signal values are well calibrated against the strategy and\narchitecture context: E002's `local_runnability: 0` (Gateway API routing behavior needs a\nlive cluster) and the branch plugin epics' `repo_access: 0` (plugin source repo not\ndocumented in architecture context — an honest limitation) are appropriately conservative;\nBRANCH-B-E006's `adapter_pattern: 0` (new configuration surface, no reference\nimplementation) and BRANCH-B-E005's `change_specificity: 0` (refactor surface known only\nafter the investigation's factoring plan) are justified. `architecture_claims: 1` rationales\ncite the covering architecture-context files (mlflow.md, mlflow-operator.md) rather than\nasserting \"no architecture claims\" — the strategy's premises (flags in the RHOAI container\nentrypoint, Helm-in-operator pattern, conditional CRD discovery) are corroborated, so the\ncalibration check passes. No arithmetic or threshold checking performed, per instructions.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\nEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy: E003's ACs enumerate the exact\nstartup matrix and HTTP response codes (200/403/503) from the strategy's acceptance\nscenarios 1-4; E004's ACs cover strategy scenarios 5-6 plus regression, conditional CRD\ndiscovery, and the misconfiguration-rejection risk mitigation; the branch and Investigation\nepics have document- and behavior-verifiable ACs. No rule-mandated AC is missing: there are\nno replacement epics (backwards-compatibility ACs are present anyway on E003, E004, and\nBRANCH-B-E006), no konflux chain, and no docs-authoring epic (Documentation Support is\n\"Pending human review\", not Yes — Rule 11 correctly not triggered). No externally-controlled\nprecondition is misfiled as an AC: E004's \"rendered artifacts-only instance starts\nsuccessfully with the combined flag set\" gates on a sibling epic within the same\nstrategy/team and is the sanctioned Rule 13 AC-gate coupling, not the soft-AC pattern. No\nepic body references sibling epics by bare draft ID — verified by grep; `E00x`-style\nreferences appear only in structured frontmatter fields as full `RHAISTRAT-8009-Exxx` keys.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\nThe combined epic set accounts for the full strategy scope: all three affected components,\nall 8 HLRs, and all 6 strategy acceptance scenarios (1-4 in E003's ACs, 5-6 in E004's ACs).\nStrategy-level context is carried forward, not dropped: all four named risks have\nmitigations scoped into epics (path-resolution risk → E003's S3-scoped AC + Health Warning;\nplugin coupling → E001/BRANCH-B-E005; split-deployment complexity → E004's validation AC;\nlatency → E003's short-TTL caching); all five \"needs validation\" assumptions are addressed\n(including the Helm-chart second-Deployment assumption as an explicit E004 AC); and both\nPM-owned open questions are recorded as Health Warnings with non-structural scope handling.\nThe NFRs (security/fail-closed, backwards compatibility, performance) map to E003/E004 ACs.\nConditional branches cover all bounded outcomes for both Investigations (E001: A and B both\nhave branch epics; E002: outcome A covered by main-plan E004's route-level scope, outcome B\nby BRANCH-B-E006). The Staff Engineer input is applied verbatim — verified by grep that no\nepic introduces the rejected flag/CR field or prescribes SSAR mechanics. Docs-authoring\ncorrectly omitted per the Documentation Support gate status.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage