{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9ba2c60bacda364e","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n3\tepic_count: 7\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8009 — Workspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments\n10\t\n11\tStrategy: RHAISTRAT-8009 (priority: Critical, status: In Progress). Source RFE: RHAIRFE-7127.\n12\tAll affected components (MLflow, MLflow Operator, mlflow-kubernetes-plugins) are owned by a single\n13\tteam (MLflow Team), but they are distinct components in distinct repositories, so they decompose into\n14\tseparate epics per the epic boundary rules.\n15\t\n16\t## Triage\n17\t\n18\tNot below threshold: the strategy is M-L (4–7 sprints), spans three architecturally distinct\n19\tcomponents (Python tracking server, Go operator, external auth plugin package), and contains genuine\n20\tunknowns (open questions with \"needs validation\" assumptions) whose answers change downstream epic\n21\tscope. Not documentation-only: all three components require code changes.\n22\t\n23\t## Staff Engineer Input Handling\n24\t\n25\tThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section is non-empty and takes precedence. Three directives were\n26\tapplied, and the AI-generated Technical Approach in the strategy already incorporates all three\n27\t(no residual divergence to override):\n28\t\n29\t1. **No new flag** — the decomposition contains no epic introducing `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware`\n30\t   or `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware`. All server work combines the existing `--artifacts-only`\n31\t   and `--enable-workspaces` flags; all operator work propagates existing flags. Logged as an\n32\t   accepted override of the original STRAT proposal.\n33\t2. **Operator work is a split deployment, not a config toggle** — the operator epic's scope is the\n34\t   full split-server topology (dedicated Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute + artifact traffic\n35\t   routing from the main tracking deployment), not a CR-field toggle.\n36\t3. **SSAR detail is over-specified** — no epic prescribes SelfSubjectAccessReview against\n37\t   `mlflow.kubeflow.org` pseudo-resources or any other Kubernetes API mechanics for authorization.\n38\t   Epics state that workspace authorization reuses the existing auth plugin infrastructure;\n39\t   kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and `--app-name kubernetes-auth` are unchanged.\n40\t\n41\t## Component Mapping (canonical Jira components)\n42\t\n43\tThe canonical list in `.context/rhai-components.txt` contains `MLflow` but has no separate entries\n44\tfor the operator or the plugin package. Mappings used (logged per the component-name constraint):\n45\t\n46\t| Strategy component | Canonical component | Note |\n47\t|---|---|---|\n48\t| MLflow | MLflow | Exact match |\n49\t| MLflow Operator | MLflow | No canonical \"MLflow Operator\" entry; the operator manages MLflow instances, so `MLflow` is the closest canonical name. Operator work (Go, red-hat-data-services/mlflow-operator repo) is tracked under `MLflow`. |\n50\t| mlflow-kubernetes-plugins | MLflow | External PyPI plugin package (v1.5.0) consumed by the MLflow server; no standalone canonical entry, so plugin work is tracked under `MLflow`. |\n51\t\n52\tNo new-component onboarding chain is generated: architecture context files exist for MLflow\n53\t(mlflow.md) and the MLflow Operator (mlflow-operator.md); mlflow-kubernetes-plugins is an existing\n54\tpublished dependency of the MLflow server (documented in mlflow.md), not a component being\n55\tprovisioned into the platform.\n56\t\n57\t## Epic List\n58\t\n59\tMain plan:\n60\t\n61\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n62\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n63\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Workspace resolution initialization dependencies in the artifacts-only context | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n64\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Split-server artifact traffic routing design | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n65\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Composable workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow server | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n66\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | Split-server operator topology for the dedicated artifacts-only instance | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n67\t\n68\tConditional branch epics (attached as plan documents to their gating Investigation, not standalone\n69\tJira issues):\n70\t\n71\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority | Gate |\n72\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n73\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-A-E005 | Verify and integrate plugin workspace resolution in the artifacts-only context | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 | E001 outcome A |\n74\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E005 | Refactor plugin workspace resolution into an independent initialization path | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 | E001 outcome B |\n75\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E006 | Tracking server artifact-delegation configuration | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 | E002 outcome B |\n76\t\n77\t## Dependency DAG\n78\t\n79\t```mermaid\n80\tgraph TD\n81\t    E001[\"E001 Investigation: workspace resolution init dependencies (P0)\"]\n82\t    E002[\"E002 Investigation: split-server artifact routing design (P1)\"]\n83\t    E003[\"E003 Implementation: composable workspace-aware artifacts-only serving (P0)\"]\n84\t    E004[\"E004 Implementation: operator split-server topology (P1)\"]\n85\t    BA[\"BRANCH-A-E005 plugin verify + integrate (P0)\"]\n86\t    BB5[\"BRANCH-B-E005 plugin refactor (P0)\"]\n87\t    BB6[\"BRANCH-B-E006 server delegation config (P1)\"]\n88\t\n89\t    E001 --> E003\n90\t    E002 --> E004\n91\t    E001 --> BA\n92\t    E001 --> BB5\n93\t    E002 --> BB6\n94\t```\n95\t\n96\tArrows point from dependency to dependent (`E001 --> E003` means E001 must complete before E003\n97\tstarts). E001 and E002 are independent roots and start in parallel.\n98\t\n99\t## DAG Justification\n100\t\n101\t| Edge (or non-edge) | Rule | Rationale |\n102\t|---|---|---|\n103\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gate) | Whether the workspace provider / workspace store can be initialized without tracking and model registry stores determines the server's initialization approach for combined-flag mode. True gate, action `rewrite`: if resolution is coupled to full server initialization, E003's initialization path is reworked to use the independent initialization path delivered by the plugin refactoring. |\n104\t| E001 → BRANCH-A-E005, E001 → BRANCH-B-E005 | Rule 3 (bounded outcomes, conditional branches) | E001 has two bounded outcomes that change what the plugin epic does: (A) resolution is independently initializable — verification and integration only; (B) resolution is coupled to tracking-store initialization — refactor into an independent initialization path. Expressed as conditional branch epics under the gating Investigation. |\n105\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation gate) | The routing design determines the HTTPRoute design for both instances and how the main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic. True gate, action `rewrite`: the operator epic's routing implementation follows whichever mechanism the investigation selects. |\n106\t| E002 → BRANCH-B-E006 | Rule 3 (bounded outcomes, conditional branches) | Outcome A: routing is resolved at the Gateway/HTTPRoute level and no server-side change is needed (branch epic does not exist). Outcome B: the tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations — an additional server-side epic, captured as a branch under the gating Investigation. |\n107\t| No edge E001 ↔ E002 | Rule 14 (parallel) | Independent unknowns in different code areas (plugin/workspace-store initialization vs. ingress routing design); neither answer inputs the other. Both investigations start immediately. |\n108\t| No edge E003 → E004 | Rules 14 + 13 (configuration reference, not build-against artifact) | MLflow server (Python) and the operator (Go) live in different repositories with no shared build artifacts. The operator renders the server's CLI flags into a Deployment spec — a configuration reference consumed at execution time, not an API/CRD/library the operator code builds against. The coupling is expressed as an acceptance criterion on E004 (\"the rendered artifacts-only instance starts successfully with the combined flag set\"), never as a DAG edge, so server and operator work proceed in parallel. |\n109\t| No edge E004 → E003 | Rule 14 (parallel) | The server-side flag composition does not depend on the operator: the workspace-aware artifacts-only server can be started and validated directly with CLI flags (the strategy's acceptance scenarios 1–4 do this), independent of operator deployment. |\n110\t| No edge plugin epics ↔ E003 | Rule 14 (parallel, AC-gated integration) | The server delegates authorization to the existing plugin interface (entry-point group), which exists today; the plugin branch work and the server work proceed in parallel in different repositories. Integration is verified by acceptance criteria on both sides (server: workspace validation denies with 403; plugin: resolution works with stores uninitialized). |\n111\t| No edge E002 → E003 | Rule 14 (parallel) | The server's combined-flag behavior is independent of how artifact traffic is routed between instances. |\n112\t| No standalone integration-test epic | Boundary rules (validation folded into ACs) | Cross-component end-to-end validation (split-server routing with workspace validation) is carried as acceptance criteria on the operator epic, which assembles the topology; per-component scenario coverage (multi-tenant allow/deny, single-tenant regression, fail-closed) is carried as acceptance criteria on the server and plugin epics. All work is one team, so a separate test epic would fragment one logical validation surface. |\n113\t| No docs-authoring epic | Boundary rules | The strategy's Documentation Support gate is \"Pending human review\" (not Yes) and no HLR calls for substantive content authoring; the only documentation in scope (limitations of non-standard artifact roots, denial-log context) falls out of the code changes and is folded into the originating epics as scope/acceptance criteria. |\n114\t\n115\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n116\t\n117\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n118\t|---|---|---|---|\n119\t| HLR-1: `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` + `--workspace-store-uri` | P0 | E003 | Server-side flag composition; no new flag (Staff Engineer Input). |\n120\t| HLR-2: artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403 | P0 | E003, BRANCH-A-E005 / BRANCH-B-E005 | Server implements request validation and delegates authorization to the plugin infrastructure; the plugin branch epic (selected by the E001 outcome) makes resolution work in the artifacts-only context. |\n121\t| HLR-3: fail-closed when workspace store unreachable | P0 | E003 | Deny rather than serve without validation. |\n122\t| HLR-4: single-tenant / no-`--enable-workspaces` deployments retain current behavior | P0 | E003, E004 (regression scope) | Backwards-compatibility regression coverage at the flag level (E003) and for CR-driven non-split deployments (E004). |\n123\t| HLR-5: operator deploys dedicated artifacts-only instance (Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute) | P1 | E004 | Split-server topology per Staff Engineer Input. |\n124\t| HLR-6: main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated instance | P1 | E004, BRANCH-B-E006 (conditional) | Routing mechanism selected by E002; if the tracking server itself needs a delegation configuration, that work is the outcome-B branch epic. |\n125\t| HLR-7: auth plugin workspace resolution works in artifacts-only context without tracking stores | P1 | BRANCH-A-E005 / BRANCH-B-E005 | Raised to P0 by inheritance: the plugin work transitively enables the P0 workspace-validation capability (HLR-2). Scope selected by the E001 outcome (verify vs. refactor). |\n126\t| HLR-8: audit logging for workspace access denials via server logs | P2 | E003 (bundled) | Bundled, not split: denial logging is emitted from the same workspace-validation denial path in artifact request handling that HLR-2/HLR-3 build — a separate epic would make two passes over the same call sites and has no independent user value apart from the validation feature. Splitting would cost duplicate touching of the denial path and an artificial serialization edge. |\n127\t\n128\tAll 8 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n129\t\n130\t## Conditional Branches\n131\t\n132\t**E001 — workspace resolution initialization dependencies** has two bounded outcomes:\n133\t\n134\t- **Outcome A (decoupled)**: the plugin's workspace resolution and the `kubernetes://` workspace store\n135\t  initialize without tracking/model registry stores. Plugin work is verification and integration\n136\t  only → `BRANCH-A-E005`.\n137\t- **Outcome B (coupled)**: resolution depends on full tracking server initialization. Plugin work is\n138\t  a refactoring that factors workspace resolution into an independent initialization path →\n139\t  `BRANCH-B-E005` (the risk table's mitigation).\n140\t\n141\t**E002 — split-server artifact traffic routing design** has two bounded outcomes:\n142\t\n143\t- **Outcome A (route-level)**: artifact traffic is split at the Gateway/HTTPRoute level; no\n144\t  tracking-server change. Only main-plan E004 implements routing.\n145\t- **Outcome B (server delegation)**: the tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate\n146\t  artifact operations → additional branch epic `BRANCH-B-E006`.\n147\t\n148\tBranch epics are excluded from the main-plan critical path. `epic_count` = 7 counts all epics\n149\t(4 main-plan + 3 branch); main-plan count is 4.\n150\t\n151\t## Health Warnings\n152\t\n153\t- **Priority tension on the operator epic (non-blocking)**: the strategy assigns P1 to the operator\n154\t  HLRs (HLR-5/6) while the business need — tenant-isolated artifact serving in deployed multi-tenant\n155\t  MLflow — only reaches users once the operator deploys the split topology. The P0 server capability\n156\t  is independently testable without the operator (hence E004 stays P1 by the inheritance rule), but\n157\t  if product management considers the *deployed* capability the P0 outcome, E004 (and E002) should be\n158\t  re-prioritized to P0. Human verification requested.\n159\t- **Open question: acceptable per-request latency (Owner: PM/Engineering)** — does not gate the\n160\t  decomposition: the strategy's risk mitigation directs implementing short-TTL caching of workspace\n161\t  membership decisions regardless, so caching is scoped inside E003. If PM later rules caching\n162\t  unnecessary at launch, it is trimmable scope within the same epic, not a structural change.\n163\t- **Open question: audit telemetry channel (Owner: PM/Security)** — HLR-8 already specifies server\n164\t  logs, so audit logging is scoped as server-log emission in E003. If PM/Security selects a\n165\t  different channel (OTLP, Kubernetes audit log), the scope adjusts within the same epic;\n166\t  non-structural.\n167\t- **Strategy-level process gates pending**: Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding, License\n168\t  Validation, and other prerequisites are all \"Pending human review\". These are strategy-level human\n169\t  gates, not decomposition structure; they are reflected as unfavorable human-process-gate signals on\n170\t  the implementation epics.\n171\t- **Scope trap watched, not triggered**: the artifact path resolution risk (paths may not map to\n172\t  experiment IDs in all backends) is bounded by scoping initial implementation to the S3 artifact\n173\t  store with the standard `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` layout and documenting limitations\n174\t  for non-standard artifact roots — an AC on E003, consistent with the strategy's own mitigation.\n175\t- **Design doc / ADR \"to be created\"** (Supporting Documentation): the design decisions are captured\n176\t  here and in the two Investigation epics' deliverables; a formal design doc/ADR remains outstanding\n177\t  strategy work outside this decomposition's scope.\n178\t\n179\t## Tiered Delivery\n180\t\n181\tNot applicable. All dependencies are internal (workspace store, auth plugin, Gateway API,\n182\tkube-rbac-proxy all exist in the platform per the strategy's dependency table and architecture\n183\tcontext); there is no external dependency with uncertain timing, so no Tier 1 / Tier 2 split is\n184\tneeded. The conditional branches above are uncertainty gates, not delivery tiers.\n185\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9996d9462f28a3d6","agent_id":"ab8810fc282738a5e","session_id":"16006724-b396-4d55-996f-e395dcbbe214","uuid":"65e2d1c3-8d4c-496f-a8c0-5b52f6ff92f5","timestamp":"2026-10-26T13:46:01.041Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9ba2c60bacda364e","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
