{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bbdd74c3039a8656","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E003\n3\ttitle: Composable workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow server\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n5\tcomponent: MLflow\n6\tteam: MLflow Team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\n11\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\n12\tgate_failure_impact:\n13\t  action: rewrite\n14\t  fallback_approach: If the workspace-resolution initialization-dependency investigation\n15\t    finds resolution coupled to full tracking-server initialization, the workspace-provider\n16\t    initialization approach in this epic is reworked to use the independent initialization\n17\t    path delivered by the plugin refactoring before workspace validation can land.\n18\tai_signals:\n19\t  change_specificity: 1\n20\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n21\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n22\t  existing_foundation: 1\n23\t  open_questions: -1\n24\t  external_dependency: 0\n25\t  human_process_gates: -1\n26\t  repo_access: 1\n27\t  architecture_claims: 1\n28\timplementation_type: null\n29\tinvestigation_signals: null\n30\tai_implementability: High\n31\tai_implementability_score: 4\n32\tjira_key: null\n33\tbranch: null\n34\t---\n35\t# Composable workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow server\n36\t\n37\t## Description\n38\t\n39\tThis epic delivers the server-side half of workspace-aware artifact serving: making the MLflow\n40\ttracking server's `--artifacts-only` mode composable with the existing `--enable-workspaces` flag,\n41\tso that the artifacts-only server initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate\n42\tartifact requests against tenant boundaries. No new server flag is introduced — the workspace-aware\n43\tbehavior emerges from combining existing flags (Staff Engineer / SME Input). When both flags are\n44\tset, the server requires an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`, initializes only the workspace\n45\tprovider, keeps tracking and model registry stores uninitialized, and leaves non-artifact API\n46\tendpoints disabled.\n47\t\n48\tFor each artifact request, the server resolves the artifact path to its parent experiment\n49\t(artifacts are stored under experiment-scoped paths, e.g. `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`),\n50\tlooks up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegates authorization\n51\tto the existing auth plugin infrastructure. Requests that fail workspace validation receive HTTP\n52\t403. The design is fail-closed: if the workspace store is unreachable while workspace-aware mode is\n53\tactive, artifact requests are denied rather than served without validation.\n54\t\n55\t## Scope\n56\t\n57\t- Flag composition: allow `--artifacts-only` to combine with `--enable-workspaces`; when both are\n58\t  set, require an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` (fail to start with a clear error if missing) and\n59\t  initialize only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests, without initializing\n60\t  tracking or model registry stores and without enabling non-artifact API endpoints.\n61\t- Workspace validation in artifact request handling: resolve the artifact path of each request to\n62\t  its parent experiment, look up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and\n63\t  delegate authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure; deny non-members with HTTP 403\n64\t  and an error indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n65\t- Fail-closed behavior: when workspace-aware mode is active and the workspace store is unreachable,\n66\t  deny artifact requests with an error indicating workspace validation is unavailable (HTTP 503),\n67\t  never serve without validation.\n68\t- Backwards compatibility: `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` retains current\n69\t  behavior (artifacts served on path alone, no workspace validation); each flag used individually\n70\t  retains its current behavior; no existing API contracts, CLI arguments, or configuration keys\n71\t  change meaning.\n72\t- Audit logging of workspace access denials through server logs, with the context available at the\n73\t  denial point (workspace, experiment, requesting identity) — the P2 audit requirement is delivered\n74\t  here because it is emitted from the same denial path this epic builds.\n75\t- Short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions per artifact request path, consistent with\n76\t  caching patterns already used in the tracking server's auth path (per the strategy's risk\n77\t  mitigation for per-request resolution latency).\n78\t- Initial artifact-path resolution scoped to the S3 artifact store (the standard RHOAI\n79\t  configuration) with the well-defined `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` structure;\n80\t  limitations for non-standard artifact roots documented.\n81\t- Test coverage: multi-tenant allow/deny scenarios, single-tenant and no-workspace regression,\n82\t  fail-closed edge cases (unreachable workspace store), flag-combination startup matrix.\n83\t\n84\t## Acceptance Criteria\n85\t\n86\t- A server started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://`\n87\t  initializes the workspace provider without initializing tracking or model registry stores, and\n88\t  non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled — verified by startup behavior and endpoint probes.\n89\t- A server started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces` but without `--workspace-store-uri`\n90\t  fails to start with a clear error message.\n91\t- A user who is a member of workspace A requesting an artifact belonging to an experiment in\n92\t  workspace A receives HTTP 200 with the correct artifact content.\n93\t- A user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B requesting an artifact belonging to an\n94\t  experiment in workspace B receives HTTP 403 with an error message indicating insufficient\n95\t  workspace permissions.\n96\t- With the workspace-aware artifacts-only server running and the workspace store (Kubernetes API)\n97\t  unreachable, artifact requests are denied with HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is\n98\t  unavailable (fail-closed).\n99\t- A server started with `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` serves artifacts without\n100\t  workspace validation (HTTP 200 regardless of workspace membership) — current behavior preserved.\n101\t- Each flag used individually retains current behavior, demonstrated by the existing server test\n102\t  suite passing without modification.\n103\t- Workspace access denials are recorded in server logs with the denial context available at the\n104\t  point of denial.\n105\t- Workspace membership decision caching is implemented with a short TTL, and expired entries are\n106\t  re-validated (covered by tests).\n107\t- Artifact path resolution is implemented for the S3-standard `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`\n108\t  layout, and limitations for non-standard artifact roots are documented.\n109\t\n110\t## HLR Traceability\n111\t\n112\t- HLR-1 (P0): `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri`.\n113\t- HLR-2 (P0): artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403.\n114\t- HLR-3 (P0): fail-closed behavior when the workspace store is unreachable.\n115\t- HLR-4 (P0): single-tenant / no-`--enable-workspaces` deployments retain current behavior.\n116\t- HLR-8 (P2, bundled): audit logging for workspace access denials through server logs — bundled\n117\t  rather than split because denial logging is emitted from the same workspace-validation denial path\n118\t  this epic builds for HLR-2/HLR-3; a separate epic would make two passes over the same call sites\n119\t  and has no independent user value apart from the validation feature.\n120\t\n121\t## Risks / Notes\n122\t\n123\t- This epic is gated by the workspace-resolution initialization-dependency investigation: its\n124\t  workspace-provider initialization approach follows that investigation's determination. If\n125\t  workspace resolution proves coupled to full tracking-server initialization, the initialization\n126\t  path here is reworked to use the independent initialization path delivered by the plugin\n127\t  refactoring before workspace validation can land.\n128\t- The workspace validation delegates to the existing auth plugin infrastructure\n129\t  (`--app-name kubernetes-auth`); this epic does not prescribe Kubernetes API authorization\n130\t  mechanics, which remain an mlflow-kubernetes-plugins implementation detail (Staff Engineer /\n131\t  SME Input).\n132\t- The acceptable per-request latency target is an open question owned by PM/Engineering; caching is\n133\t  implemented per the strategy's risk mitigation regardless, so the open answer trims or keeps scope\n134\t  within this epic rather than changing structure.\n135\t- Component mapping: canonical Jira component `MLflow`.\n136\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a78bd5ae81820315","agent_id":"ae780ac218091a484","session_id":"16006724-b396-4d55-996f-e395dcbbe214","uuid":"a069e404-0769-41c9-927c-b9041b615542","timestamp":"2026-10-26T13:47:13.450Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-bbdd74c3039a8656","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8009"}
