{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-806c2a54f1533229","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E004\n3\ttitle: Split-server operator topology for the dedicated artifacts-only instance\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n5\tcomponent: MLflow\n6\tteam: MLflow Team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P1\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E002\n11\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8009-E002\n12\tgate_failure_impact:\n13\t  action: rewrite\n14\t  fallback_approach: If the split-server routing design investigation selects a different\n15\t    routing mechanism than assumed, the routing implementation in this epic is rewritten\n16\t    to follow the selected mechanism; if the investigation determines the tracking\n17\t    server needs a delegation configuration, that server-side change is planned as\n18\t    conditional branch work under the investigation rather than folded into this operator\n19\t    epic.\n20\tai_signals:\n21\t  change_specificity: 1\n22\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n23\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n24\t  existing_foundation: 1\n25\t  open_questions: -1\n26\t  external_dependency: 0\n27\t  human_process_gates: -1\n28\t  repo_access: 1\n29\t  architecture_claims: 1\n30\timplementation_type: null\n31\tinvestigation_signals: null\n32\tai_implementability: High\n33\tai_implementability_score: 4\n34\tjira_key: null\n35\tbranch: null\n36\t---\n37\t# Split-server operator topology for the dedicated artifacts-only instance\n38\t\n39\t## Description\n40\t\n41\tThis epic delivers the operator-side half of workspace-aware artifact serving: the MLflow operator\n42\tdeploys a dedicated artifacts-only MLflow instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and\n43\tHTTPRoute, and configures the main tracking deployment to route artifact traffic to that dedicated\n44\tinstance. This is a split-server topology — the main tracking server handles experiment tracking,\n45\tmodel registry, and prompt registry, while the artifacts-only instance handles artifact serving\n46\twith workspace validation — not a single-deployment configuration toggle (Staff Engineer / SME\n47\tInput).\n48\t\n49\tThe operator already uses a Helm-in-operator pattern: the MLflow CR spec is converted to Helm\n50\tvalues, rendered through embedded chart templates, and applied via Server-Side Apply. The split\n51\tdeployment adds new chart templates for the artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute, and\n52\tupdates the main tracking Deployment to configure artifact traffic routing. Workspace configuration\n53\t(`--enable-workspaces`, `--workspace-store-uri`, `--app-name kubernetes-auth`) is propagated to the\n54\tartifacts-only instance using the same configuration mechanisms already used for the main tracking\n55\tdeployment. Gateway API HTTPRoute resources are created conditionally based on runtime CRD\n56\tdiscovery, consistent with the operator's existing pattern for optional dependencies.\n57\t\n58\t## Scope\n59\t\n60\t- New Helm chart templates for the dedicated artifacts-only instance: Deployment, Service, and\n61\t  HTTPRoute, separate from the main tracking deployment's resources.\n62\t- A CR configuration surface (within the existing MLflow CR) that enables the split-server\n63\t  artifacts-only deployment — exact field naming is an implementing-team decision; no new\n64\t  server-level flag or workspace-aware CR field is introduced (Staff Engineer / SME Input).\n65\t- Propagation of workspace configuration to the artifacts-only instance using the existing\n66\t  configuration mechanisms, so the dedicated instance runs with the combined flag set\n67\t  (`--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes:// --app-name\n68\t  kubernetes-auth --serve-artifacts`).\n69\t- Configuration of the main tracking deployment to route artifact traffic to the dedicated\n70\t  artifacts-only instance, following the routing mechanism selected by the split-server routing\n71\t  design investigation (route-level traffic splitting versus a tracking-server delegation\n72\t  configuration).\n73\t- kube-rbac-proxy sidecar deployed on the artifacts-only instance identically to the main tracking\n74\t  deployment — the authentication layer is unchanged and works identically for both instances.\n75\t- Conditional HTTPRoute creation for the artifacts-only instance based on runtime Gateway API CRD\n76\t  discovery, per the operator's existing optional-dependency pattern.\n77\t- Operator validation that rejects MLflow CR configurations where the artifacts-only instance would\n78\t  be deployed without workspace configuration (misconfiguration that could let artifact requests\n79\t  bypass workspace validation), with a clear error/status condition.\n80\t- Regression coverage: single-deployment (non-split) MLflow CRs reconcile exactly as before;\n81\t  end-to-end coverage for the split topology including artifact traffic routing and workspace\n82\t  validation through the deployed instances.\n83\t\n84\t## Acceptance Criteria\n85\t\n86\t- Given an MLflow CR with split-server artifact serving enabled, reconciling the CR creates a\n87\t  dedicated artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute separate from the main tracking\n88\t  Deployment — verified by the presence of distinct Deployment resources with the correct flag\n89\t  combinations.\n90\t- The artifacts-only Deployment's container command includes `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces\n91\t  --workspace-store-uri kubernetes:// --app-name kubernetes-auth --serve-artifacts`, verified by\n92\t  inspecting the rendered Deployment spec.\n93\t- The rendered artifacts-only instance starts successfully with the combined flag set (this gates on\n94\t  the server-side combined-flag capability being available; it is tracked here as an acceptance\n95\t  criterion at execution time rather than a work dependency, so operator and server work proceed in\n96\t  parallel).\n97\t- The dedicated instance runs the kube-rbac-proxy sidecar configured identically to the main\n98\t  tracking deployment, and authentication at the ingress level works identically for both.\n99\t- HTTPRoute resources for the artifacts-only instance are created only when the Gateway API CRDs are\n100\t  discovered at runtime, and the operator continues to function on clusters without them.\n101\t- Artifact traffic sent to the main tracking deployment is served by the dedicated artifacts-only\n102\t  instance with workspace validation applied (end-to-end through the deployed split topology).\n103\t- The operator rejects MLflow CR configurations that enable the artifacts-only instance without\n104\t  workspace configuration, with a clear error or status condition, so misconfiguration cannot\n105\t  silently bypass workspace validation.\n106\t- Single-deployment MLflow CRs reconcile exactly as before (regression suite green).\n107\t- The chart rendering supports the second Deployment within the existing Helm-in-operator chart\n108\t  architecture, or any required chart restructuring is completed within this epic (validating the\n109\t  strategy's assumption).\n110\t\n111\t## HLR Traceability\n112\t\n113\t- HLR-5 (P1): operator deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment with its\n114\t  own Service and HTTPRoute.\n115\t- HLR-6 (P1): main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only\n116\t  instance (mechanism selected by the split-server routing design investigation).\n117\t- HLR-4 (P0, regression scope): deployments without the split topology retain current behavior —\n118\t  covered here as CR-driven-deployment regression for the same backwards-compatibility requirement\n119\t  the server epic covers at the flag level.\n120\t\n121\t## Risks / Notes\n122\t\n123\t- This epic is gated by the split-server artifact traffic routing design investigation: the routing\n124\t  implementation follows whichever mechanism that investigation selects. If the investigation\n125\t  determines the tracking server needs a delegation configuration, that server-side change is\n126\t  planned as conditional branch work under the investigation, not folded into this operator epic.\n127\t- Split-deployment operational complexity is a named strategy risk (two Deployments, two Services,\n128\t  two HTTPRoutes, routing configuration). Mitigations in scope: declarative single-CR management and\n129\t  the operator validation acceptance criterion above.\n130\t- No DAG dependency on the server-side combined-flag work: different repositories with no shared\n131\t  build artifacts; the coupling is the acceptance criterion above (\"rendered artifacts-only instance\n132\t  starts successfully with the combined flag set\"), never a blocking edge.\n133\t- Component mapping: canonical Jira component `MLflow` (no canonical \"MLflow Operator\" entry\n134\t  exists; the operator manages MLflow instances, so `MLflow` is the closest canonical name). The\n135\t  epic's work spans the strategy components \"MLflow Operator\" (chart templates, reconciliation,\n136\t  validation) and \"MLflow\" (main tracking deployment routing configuration).\n137\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a78bd5ae81820315","agent_id":"ae780ac218091a484","session_id":"16006724-b396-4d55-996f-e395dcbbe214","uuid":"1ace692c-1b97-47dc-a942-675cc0a6d3b2","timestamp":"2026-10-26T13:47:16.216Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-806c2a54f1533229","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8009"}
